    "body": "\r\n## Problem\r\n\r\nCloses #42046 \r\n`TSNode:iter_children()` always starts from the first direct child.\r\n\r\nConsumers interested in children near a source position must iterate over every\r\npreceding child in Lua. This requires one Lua/C iterator call per skipped child\r\nand becomes expensive for flat nodes with many direct children.\r\n\r\nTree-sitter already supports seeking a `TSTreeCursor` to the first child at or\r\nafter a point, but this functionality is not exposed by the current Lua API.\r\n\r\n## Solution\r\n\r\nAllow `TSNode:iter_children()` to accept an optional zero-based start position:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nfor child, field in node:iter_children({\r\n  start = { row, col },\r\n}) do\r\n  -- ...\r\nend\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe first result is the direct child containing the position or the first child\r\nstarting after it. A position after the last child produces an empty iterator.\r\n\r\nThe positioned form uses\r\n`ts_tree_cursor_goto_first_child_for_point()` followed by\r\n`ts_tree_cursor_goto_next_sibling()`. Anonymous children and field names are\r\npreserved.\r\n\r\nCalls without a start position continue to use the existing index-based\r\nimplementation. This preserves compatibility and avoids additional cursor\r\nallocation for small or partially consumed iterators.\r\n\r\n### Alternatives considered\r\n\r\n- **Skip preceding children in Lua:** requires one Lua/C call for every skipped\r\n  child, so its cost grows with the start position.\r\n- **Binary-search `node:child(index)`:** avoids exposing every skipped child to\r\n  Lua, but repeatedly performs indexed Tree-sitter lookups. Tree-sitter does not\r\n  document an O(1) complexity guarantee for `ts_node_child()`, and it already\r\n  provides a cursor operation specialized for seeking by point.\r\n- **Use `ts_node_first_child_for_byte()`:** finds a child but does not provide\r\n  its field name or cursor state for efficiently continuing through siblings.\r\n- **Expose the complete `TSTreeCursor` API:** potentially useful as a future\r\n  API, but substantially broader than the range-aware child iteration needed\r\n  here.\r\n- **Replace all child iteration with a cursor:** faster for sufficiently large\r\n  complete traversals, but slower to create and slower when only the first child\r\n  is consumed. This PR therefore keeps the existing no-options path.\r\n\r\n### Benchmark\r\n\r\nMeasured with:\r\n\r\n- Neovim `0.13.0-dev`, `RelWithDebInfo`\r\n- Tree-sitter `0.27.0`\r\n- bundled C parser\r\n- Intel Core i5-14600K\r\n- one declaration per line, producing a `translation_unit` with the stated\r\n  number of direct children\r\n\r\nEach seek benchmark starts near the middle of the root and consumes 30\r\nchildren. The baseline starts `iter_children()` at child zero and skips nodes\r\nuntil the target row. The positioned iterator starts with\r\n`iter_children({ start = { target_row, 0 } })`.\r\n\r\nMean time per operation:\r\n\r\n| Direct children | Linear iteration | Positioned iterator | Speedup |\r\n| --------------: | ---------------: | ------------------: | ------: |\r\n|             100 |        0.0268 ms |           0.0072 ms |   3.73x |\r\n|           1,000 |        0.1934 ms |           0.0069 ms |  27.89x |\r\n|           5,000 |        1.0367 ms |           0.0070 ms | 147.60x |\r\n|          20,000 |        4.7608 ms |           0.0071 ms | 669.76x |\r\n\r\nFor a root with 20,000 direct children, moving the target toward the end\r\nincreases the baseline cost while the positioned iterator remains nearly\r\nconstant:\r\n\r\n| Target position | Linear iteration | Positioned iterator |   Speedup |\r\n| --------------: | ---------------: | ------------------: | --------: |\r\n|              0% |        0.0105 ms |           0.0064 ms |     1.64x |\r\n|             25% |        2.2902 ms |           0.0073 ms |   312.38x |\r\n|             50% |        4.7509 ms |           0.0073 ms |   649.44x |\r\n|             90% |        8.7961 ms |           0.0071 ms | 1,243.24x |\r\n\r\nThese are empirical results, not claims about Tree-sitter's asymptotic\r\ncomplexity.\r\n\r\nA separate microbenchmark showed why the existing no-options path is retained:\r\n\r\n| Workload                          | Existing iterator | Cursor iterator |\r\n| :-------------------------------- | ----------------: | --------------: |\r\n| Create an empty iterator          |             60 ns |          155 ns |\r\n| Consume all 3 children            |            518 ns |          641 ns |\r\n| Consume all 10 children           |          1,740 ns |        1,494 ns |\r\n| Consume the first of 100 children |            326 ns |          412 ns |\r\n\r\n### Benchmark reproduction\r\n\r\nThe benchmark can be reproduced with a `RelWithDebInfo` build by generating\r\nbuffers of independent C declarations:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nlocal lines = {}\r\nfor i = 1, child_count do\r\n  lines[i] = ('int variable_%d;'):format(i)\r\nend\r\n\r\nvim.api.nvim_buf_set_lines(0, 0, -1, false, lines)\r\n\r\nlocal root = vim.treesitter.get_parser(0, 'c'):parse(true)[1]:root()\r\nassert(root:child_count() == child_count)\r\n```\r\n\r\nAfter one warm-up iteration and a full garbage collection, use `vim.uv.hrtime()`\r\nto measure these workloads repeatedly:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\n-- Baseline: visit and discard preceding children in Lua.\r\nfor child in root:iter_children() do\r\n  if child:end_() >= target_row then\r\n    -- Consume 30 matching children.\r\n  end\r\nend\r\n\r\n-- Positioned form.\r\nlocal iter = root:iter_children({ start = { target_row, 0 } })\r\n-- Consume 30 children from iter.\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe benchmark was run with:\r\n\r\n```sh\r\n./build/bin/nvim --clean --headless -l /tmp/nvim_ts_child_iterator_bench.lua\r\n```\r\n\r\nBefore timing, the script compares node IDs, node types, and field names from\r\nboth workloads to verify equivalent results.\r\n\r\n### Tests\r\n\r\nThe functional tests cover:\r\n\r\n- unchanged behavior with empty options;\r\n- a point inside a later child;\r\n- a point between children;\r\n- a point inside the body;\r\n- a point after the node;\r\n- anonymous children;\r\n- preservation of field names.\r\n\r\nValidated locally with the focused Tree-sitter functional test, C/Lua\r\nformatting, Lua lint, direct `clang-tidy`, generated documentation, and\r\ndocumentation lint.\r\n",
